DINNER_CREATIVE — lessons from the field

File: openOODA/DINNER_CREATIVE.oot
The sidebar. The why behind the what. Academy-style flair.

LOGLINE
A kit that eats its own cooking discovers that clarity is a feature,
not a tax.

SETUP
Every file is a scene. Every function is a beat. Every name is a
contract with the reader who comes after you — including future you,
who has forgotten everything and remembers nothing.

WHY STORIES MATTER
Code is told twice. Once to the machine, which forgives nothing and
remembers everything. Once to the human, who forgets everything and
forgives nothing. The academy-style header is the second telling. It
costs lines. It pays back in hours saved, bugs avoided, and onboarding
rounds skipped. The overhead is per file. The savings are per read.
The read happens more often than the write. Bet on the read.

INTENT IS THE FIRST DELIVERABLE
A function name that does not reveal intent is a bug wearing a
disguise. The body can be perfect; the name will mislead. We name
twice if we must: once to be honest, once to be kind.

BEATS — LESSONS LEARNED
- Every magic number is a confession. It admits you didn't name it.
- If you can't explain it in one sentence, you don't understand it yet.
- A function name is a promise. Don't break it. Don't broaden it. Don't
  silently narrow it.
- Comments are decay. Code is growth. Prefer to delete the comment and
  rewrite the code so the comment becomes unnecessary.
- The canonical form is the long form. The short form is a child of
  the canonical. Never the other way around.
- The linter is the law. If the linter tolerates it, the kit tolerates
  it. If you want mercy, change the linter.
- CAPs are the truth of an op, not the body. The body serves the CAP;
  the CAP does not serve the body.
- 256 lines is a constraint, not a prison. Constraints breed craft.
  Filth breeds at infinity.

PHILOSOPHY
The kit's view: code is a story told to humans and executed by
machines. Both audiences deserve respect. The migration from comment-
soup to academy-style is one act in a longer play: make the story
readable so the execution can be trusted. When the story and the
execution agree, the system is sound. When they disagree, the bug is
in the story. Always in the story.

THE READER IS THE CUSTOMER
The compiler is a hostile reviewer with perfect memory and zero
patience. The human reader is a generous reviewer with no memory and
boundless patience — but only until they close the tab. Write for the
generous one. The hostile one will catch what they miss.

WHAT WE'D DO WITH MORE TIME
- Auto-generate short-form from canonical via a deterministic
  compressor. One source of truth. Two readers. Zero drift.
- Type-check the academy style with `trait IsAcademy { ... }`. Make the
  form a compile-time fact, not a code-review hope.
- Cross-reference the Rollup to per-file diffs. A reader of the
  Rollup should jump to the scene in one click.
- Add a "story-points" ticket to the linter. A file that breaks the
  academy style should cost points, not just a frown.

CODA
The academy award is a ceremony. Our ceremony is the diff. Every
file that earns its header earns its keep. Every file that doesn't
is a draft with a title. We ship the drafts once. Then we title them.

— end of creative sidebar —